Good — `%aI` gives ISO 8601 strict format, which is what we set in `GIT_AUTHOR_DATE`. The dates should parse correctly.

The real issue is likely the ordering — git log returns commits in reverse-chronological order (newest first). The orchestrator processes commits newest → oldest. When we process commit "remove fetchUser" (Jan 15) first, the tombstone goes into `pendingGrace`. Then we process "add fetchUser" (Jan 1) — but that's the original *addition*, not a re-addition. The grace filter correctly doesn't trigger. Then "restore fetchUser" (Jan 10) — but we never see it because git log newest-first means Jan 15 comes before Jan 10. Wait, actually Jan 20 (restore) → Jan 15 (delete) → Jan 10 (re-add within grace). Let me re-read the grace test scenario:

The grace test: add Jan 1 → delete Jan 5 → restore Jan 10 (within grace). Git processes Jan 10 first, then Jan 5, then Jan 1. When processing Jan 5 (delete), tombstone enters pendingGrace. Then Jan 1 (add) — `applyGrace` checks if added fingerprints match and if the commit date is within graceDays of buriedAt. But Jan 1 is BEFORE Jan 5 — `daysDiff` is negative. So the condition `daysDiff < 0` exits. 

The problem is that git processes in reverse order (newest first), so the "restore" commit (Jan 10) comes BEFORE the "delete" commit (Jan 5). The tombstone doesn't exist yet when we process the restore. The grace filter can only work if we also look forward — but we're processing backwards.

This is a fundamental ordering issue. The grace filter needs to look at commits that come AFTER the deletion in real time, but git delivers them in reverse order. The solution: either process git history forward (oldest first by using `--reverse`), or buffer all commits first.

Looking at the design: the spec says "after each commit, run grace-filter: if any pending tombstone's `buriedAt` is within `config.graceDays` of the current commit date, and the commit's added fingerprints match it at ≥ `config.moveThreshold`, remove that tombstone." This only makes sense if we process commits oldest-first.

